00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:14.030
You can easily combine these.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:18.590
If you want to set up a pipeline in the cloud
to combine both datasets it is easy to do

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:24.409
and have your datafiles in a single location
which can be an easier way to deal with some

00:04:24.409 --> 00:04:26.029
of the data.

00:04:26.029 --> 00:04:32.370
I mentioned the new data format the SRA aligned
reads.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.370
What are those.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:34.800
The compressed data format.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:42.060
It is in some way similar to existing SRA
data in that it can be used with the SRA toolkit

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:48.599
to dump new data formats but it has new data
that is not available anywhere else.

00:04:48.599 --> 00:04:53.830
Briefly, contigs were created from runs in
SRA that contained coronavirus kmers

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:55.930
that are part of our taxonomy analysis tool.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:00.680
That uses a kmer approach to identify the
origin of read in a run.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:08.400
Any run that I had at least 100 hits to Coronaviridae
or child taxa was selected for analysis and

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.009
a few other selection criteria were applied
to those like minimum read length and sequencing

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:19.729
platform, and contigs work created from that
set of runs, via guided assembly to the SARS-CoV-2

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:21.319
RefSeq genomic sequence.

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:27.090
If contigs were created and then those were
used mapped back to the contigs and coverage

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.780
was calculated.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:35.860
These SRA aligned read objects contain the
contigs were constructed and they also contain

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.319
the raw reads that were used to construct
the contigs.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:41.160
You can dump that however you want with the
toolkit.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.279
So you have access to the individual reads
or the contigs individually.

00:05:45.279 --> 00:05:50.300
If you have the SRA aligned read object you
can use FASTQ-dump from the toolkit to dump

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.509
FASTQ just as you would with any normal SRA
run.

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:57.849
but if what you care about is the FASTA contigs
you can get those as well.

00:05:57.849 --> 00:06:04.010
One thing I will note is a number of these
bio samples are not present in an Genbank.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:09.880
These represent a new sequence that is only
available here.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.020
If what you care about are both the contigs
and reads you can see the reads mapped onto

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.349
the contigs by using the toolkit to dump in
SAM format.

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:20.419
You can see where those aligned to the individual
contigs.

00:06:20.419 --> 00:06:25.330
In addition, the aligned reads are a smaller
size than our standard SRA format.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.029
One reason is that they do not have quality
scores.

00:06:28.029 --> 00:06:30.710
This makes them quite a bit smaller to download.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.379
A lot smaller to work with.

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:35.099
Dumps on these objects are much faster.

00:06:35.099 --> 00:06:40.889
It is just about getting you into the data
and working with it as quickly as possible.

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:47.979
Here will be the second pole questioning how
