00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:34.930
It is pretty easy to get started.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:40.949
We have these new aligned read objects with
new data but we also have new metadata you

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:42.999
can query using Athena.

00:07:42.999 --> 00:07:45.119
Since people have never used it, what is Athena?

00:07:45.119 --> 00:07:51.809
It is a query service for S3 data analysis
it allows you to directly query data stored

00:07:51.809 --> 00:07:55.439
in Amazon S3 using SQL syntax.

00:07:55.439 --> 00:07:58.479
This means there is no need to manually transform
the data for query.

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:03.360
You do not have to download a file from us
and transform it into some pseudo-database

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.900
and try to query it.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:10.389
Amazon has tools that does is for you automatically
and is really easy to be up and running in

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:11.389
a few minutes.

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:14.990
I will show you an example during set up.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:19.479
The data that we have uploaded allows you
to use Athena to search taxonomy, BLAST and

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:22.150
annotative results for aligned read contigs.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:27.740
After the contigs were created for SRA aligned
reads, the taxonomy was assessed via two methods.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.700
The first is that contigs were assessed using
our SARS-CoV-2 detection tool is a similar

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.280
kmer approach to the taxonomy tool.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.660
The second is that the contigs were checked
via megablast against a BLAST database.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.720
All of the results are available for querying
in Athena.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.380
It allows you to focus on the runs or reads
that interest you before ever downloading

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.500
the data.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:55.340
You can query these calculated metadata results
in combination with the user submitted metadata

00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:01.460
to sample information to get an even more
focused result.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:04.540
There are two separate buckets of data that
are available for querying.

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:09.300
I will talk about them and I have them in
two columns to try to make it clear two separate

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.300
sets.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:13.131
They can be queried together and you can import
them to the same database, but I will talk

00:09:13.131 --> 00:09:16.460
about them separately for conceptual reasons.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:21.570
The first is this aligned read metadata, calculated
metadata on these contigs.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:27.870
Here we have individual data on the contigs
like coverage and taxonomy id and contig length,

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:32.180
there is also taxonomy content analysis of
the contigs and you can search for records

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:37.060
based on your taxonomy level of interest for
instance, and there are also BLAST results.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:42.311
You can search for records based on hits your
accession of interest or the hit length or

00:09:42.311 --> 00:09:44.770
the percent identity or the score, for instance.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:48.790
The S3 bucket address for the metadata is
here.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:49.910
You do not have to memorize that.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:50.910
s3://sra-pub-sars-cov2-metadata-us-east-1/

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:51.910
It is also present in the documentation.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:54.300
It is just there for the record.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.930
In addition to this, we have available total
SRA metadata.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:02.670
This includes user submitted sample information
for everything in SRA.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:06.790
Where the geographical location and sample
was collected or the collection technique.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:12.310
Includes sequencing library information and
the single end or paired end.

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:15.990
As well as taxonomic content analysis for
everything in SRA.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.610
You can search based on kmer hits to your
species of interest or if you want to look

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:24.380
at a metagenome example and get a look at
what is in there by our analysis befor downloading

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:25.380
it yourself.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.360
You can do that also, just get a list of it.

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.360
This is the S3 address for that bucket.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:31.360
s3://sra-pub-metadata-us-east-1/sra/metadata/

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:32.510
Which again is just here for the record.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:35.580
These can be queried individually but you
can query them together to get a little bit

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:39.650
more of a focused filtered result and I will
show an example of that later.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:44.690
Since we have some people who are not familiar
with SQL, I will do a very brief crash course

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:50.220
on a couple of key terms to hopefully help
you follow along with the basic query.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:52.760
First is SELECT this is a command to extrat
data from a table.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.750
You are essentially telling the database what
you want to get back in your answer.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.110
A lot of times you use select star or asterisk.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:03.970
It is a wildcard to give you everything or
you might say select only the run accessions

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.090
to get back.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:07.930
FROM is a clause to specify which tables to
extract from.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:12.940
So select the run accessions from the contigs
table, let's say.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.040
And WHERE is the filter for that table.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:21.380
So Select the run accessions from the contigs
table were coverage is greater than 100 X.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:25.410
And ORDER BY allows you to order the result
by a specified column.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.260
You might order by coverage descending.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:31.390
You get the highest coverage first and on
down.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:36.061
Select the run accessions from the table where
coverage at is at least 100 X.

