00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:20.020
And then we would have communication about
that.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:26.220
I'm going to click next and finally, we need
to select the database to import this too.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:29.120
If you have existing runs, you could select
one of those from here.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.320
I'm going to create a new one.

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.140
I will call it demo 10.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:36.279
We can create and click next.

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:40.420
You can look over all of the information that
you put in if you want to go back and change,

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:42.450
you can, it looks okay to me.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.110
I will click finish.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.760
What this does is creates a crawler for you
which is in this list.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:48.720
If this is your first time you would just
have one.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:51.170
This is the one that I created.

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:54.230
You can see it has never run before but if
you look at the other crawlies I run in the

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:56.720
past you can see that these usually take one
or two minutes to run.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.510
It is not long.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:00.800
And the scanning of the data is inexpensive.

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:03.520
It should generally cost less than a dollar.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.730
And to run, if you have selected here you
can click Run crawler, and it will start running.

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:12.871
In the interest of time, I will not wait to
the couple of minutes for this to finish.

00:16:12.871 --> 00:16:15.940
I will go ahead and go back to my Athena window.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:21.290
Where I have already imported databases.

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:22.350
You can see the demo 10.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:29.980
It doesn't have tables yet but in a minute
or two it will have those tables.

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:35.680
We'll start with an aligned read table that
I have imported previously.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:37.460
A couple of quick things to show.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:41.210
You want to see which tables are available
you can click that little down arrow and it

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:45.040
will show you over here on the left and if
you want to go further to look into the contents

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:49.050
of the blastn table, it shows you the different
columns.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:54.350
The accession, length, bit score, percent
identity, etc.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:17:00.170
It can usually be a little bit easier if you
click on these three dots and click Preview

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:01.170
table.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.470
It will show you the first 10 rows.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:06.780
You can sort of see every thing that is in
it.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:08.480
You have the run succession.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.160
The name of the contigs.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:15.630
You have information about the blast hit,
the bit score, the length, the hit percent

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:20.270
identity, etc.

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:25.010
A couple other interface things I'll point
out before I go on to the demo query.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:29.130
One is that after you run a query it shows
you how long it took and how much data was

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.130
scanned.

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:36.000
How much data is scanned is how you are charged
in Athena and they have the pricing on their

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.210
website.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:39.960
It works out to five dollars per terabyte.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:45.340
All the queries I will show today will cost
pennies and I will cover that more at the

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:46.340
end.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:49.580
If you wanted to save these results, all the
results are saved to a S3 bucket for you.

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:53.750
But if you want to download the results in
csv format you can click this little button

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:54.951
and it will just download it for you.

00:17:54.951 --> 00:18:01.410
If you have a big result to analyze you can
do that and that shows up there.

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:06.000
If you have a query think you want to run
again in the future you will want to check

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:07.980
back in and see if new data is coming into
the query.

00:18:07.980 --> 00:18:12.490
Once you have it here in your query window
you can click save as.

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.930
Give it a name and description.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:20.380
When you click save it will show up here under
your saved queries.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:21.850
You can see that I have some here.

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:28.180
If I click on one of these it will bring it
up in my query window here.

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:33.260
If this is your very first time using Athena,
one more thing you want to do is go to this

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:37.050
workgroup tab you will need to have a workgroup
with a S3 bucket associated with it, which

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:39.990
is where your results will be stored.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:44.350
If you already have a workgroup with a S3
bucket associate you can select it here and

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.490
click the switch workgroup button and it will
be active.

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:52.340
If not you can create click create workgroup
give it a name and enter the address of your

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:57.850
S3 bucket and click create down here and it
will show up in this list and you can switch

00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.450
to it.

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:05.050
I will show and example of a query now.

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:06.120
This is the first query.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:07.690
I will go ahead and run this.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:09.340
This is a pretty basic one.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:14.070
We are just querying the contigs table and
this just has a list of all of the contigs

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:17.880
that are constructed with the SRA aligned
read object.

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:23.430
I'm selecting everything from the contigs
table where the tax I.D. for SARS-CoV-2 and

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:26.890
the coverage is greater than 100 X in length
is greater than 20,000.

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:29.180
You can see my result down here.

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:34.010
This just gives you a list of all the contigs
that might be of interest to you.

00:19:34.010 --> 00:19:38.410
If you are a beginner to SQL, this might be
a simple one that you can use.

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:42.470
You can toy with some of these options instead
of coverage, maybe you would want to look

00:19:42.470 --> 00:19:47.760
at hits just to get a sense of how you can
change these queries to get the results you're

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:48.840
actually interested in.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.270
You can see that it shows the data scanned.

00:19:50.270 --> 00:19:52.250
Only 42 megabytes in this case.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:56.390
This cost less than one penny, actually.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:57.890
You can do some interesting stuff.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:01.590
And I think if you combine submitted metadata
with our calculated metadata, you get more

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:02.590
interesting results.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.470
This is an example of that.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:11.640
Here I am querying both the total SRA submitted
metadata and the BLAST results for aligned

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:12.640
reads.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:19.120
I'm starting with a subquery where I am selecting
all of the run accessions from the BLAST results

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.360
where they got a BLAST results where it had
a taxid for sars-cov-2.

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:28.350
For all those accessions, I'm going to the
submitted metadata for the runs.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:32.080
I'm getting the name of the country where
this sample was collected and taking a count

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:37.920
of the country each time a country comes up
and ordering by that count.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:44.170
What we see where for all of these contigs,
where were those samples actually collected.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:45.670
We have 1700 from the USA.

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:49.150
1600 from Australia and on down.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:54.860
If, for instance, you're interested in a specific
region, like South Africa, you could drill

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:59.800
down into that more carefully, and look at
the actual data underlying that.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.620
Here is one more example.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:08.679
If, for instance, you are interested in the
South African data, I am here querying the

00:21:08.679 --> 00:21:13.660
total SRA metadata table and looking for samples
that were collected in South Africa.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:19.660
I am using all of the runs that return and
I am looking for the corresponding hits in

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:22.880
the aligned reads BLAST table.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:27.880
Any of the runs collected in South Africa,
which one had blasted SARS-CoV-2.

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:31.540
I'm looking at those and the maximum bit score.

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:36.840
You get the maximum bit score over here and
you have the contigs listed over here.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:42.310
You can see that there are a number of good
hits from this run in particular.

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:47.179
If you wanted to go and search, and get the
data for this run, you can do that pretty

00:21:47.179 --> 00:21:49.190
easily.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:54.679
I am here, bringing up a command line tool.

00:21:54.679 --> 00:21:58.200
I'm just logged into a little ec2 instance
here.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:01.880
This is really easy to get started with.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:06.220
If you have no experience with this, on our
cloud YouTube channel, we have a little video

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:09.230
to shows you how to start a ec2 instance and
get the toolkit installed.

00:22:09.230 --> 00:22:11.020
It is a little six minute video.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.220
It is very easy.

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:16.870
This is a free tier instance it doesn't cost
a lot to get started on this.

00:22:16.870 --> 00:22:23.240
Once you have a run of interest that you want
to look at the aligned reads for in the contigs,

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:27.830
you plug the run accession into this S3 address.

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:29.500
No need to memorize.

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:33.550
It is in the documentation and I will have
a link at the end.

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:39.380
Here I am using the Amazon command line interface
to copy this to my Instance.

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:45.800
When I do that, you can see it copies very
quickly and I now have this SRA aligned reads

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:46.800
files here.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:52.160
One you have that you can interact with it
using the toolkit, as you would a normal run.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:55.170
If you wanted to dump the FASTQ you can do
that.

00:22:55.170 --> 00:23:03.680
I will just just dump the first 5 reads to
standard out it will be more easily seen.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:08.460
You can dump FASTQ if you want, get the raw
reads out and do your own alignment or analysis.

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:14.280
As you can see, has placeholder quality scores
because the qualities have been removed from

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.200
these read align objects.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:22.280
If what you really care about are the fasta
contigs which may represent a new sequence,

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:25.059
they are not samples that are in Genbank.

00:23:25.059 --> 00:23:26.850
You can get just that out.

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:33.710
So use the dump-ref-fasta tool to dump the
contigs from the object.

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:37.940
I will dump this to a file here.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:44.809
The dump is quite fast and if you look at
the top of the file, you can see that we have

00:23:44.809 --> 00:23:52.730
the contig name along with the sequence and
they are all in here.

00:23:52.730 --> 00:23:59.640
If we just grep for all the contig names,
we have the entire set of contigs from just

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:02.200
this run in one single file.

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:17.970
If what you prefer is to look at the aligned
read information you can use sam-dump to dump

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:23.960
that out, standard out by default.

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:29.390
You can see on the header all the assembly
references are contigs.

00:24:29.390 --> 00:24:32.360
Here you have the reads aligned to those contigs.

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:38.040
If you want to visualize where they align
you can do that as well.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:40.790
If you want the reads in fasta you can do
that as well.

00:24:40.790 --> 00:24:45.270
You can interact with these in the toolkit
just like you would any other object in SRA.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:51.429
But you get these extra contigs not represented
anywhere else.

00:24:51.429 --> 00:24:55.190
They are designed to be small and fast to
work with.

00:24:55.190 --> 00:24:59.460
Give you access to the underlying reads and
the assembled contigs in whatever format makes

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.450
most sense for your pipeline.

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:06.799
And the Athena add-on allows you to narrow
your search to specific data of interest before

00:25:06.799 --> 00:25:09.450
you need need to download or dump the data.

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:15.680
So you don't need to download dump align the
data yourself to see if there's interest there.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.270
That's already been done.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:22.420
You can use the BLAST results or the contig
results to confirm that the data are of interest

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:26.460
to you immediately, that it have the taxonomy
hits that you are interested in and then just

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:34.160
pull the runs that you know have useful information
and start analyzing from there.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:37.790
The queries are also included in the presentation.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.980
That's available for download.

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:41.140
Quick note on cost.

00:25:41.140 --> 00:25:45.750
Predicted cost in Athena.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:50.630
The original first query which was contigs
by length cost less than a penny.

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:55.890
The other two, the geographic location by
count and the top BLAST hits cost about ten

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:56.890
cents.

00:25:56.890 --> 00:26:01.500
These are the cost based on current pricing,
that could change at any point in the future.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:04.500
As you can see it's easy and cheap to get
started.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:11.390
If this is something you are interested in
I would encourage you to do that.

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:13.200
